Chapter 1: Introduction
1.1 Motivation
The creation of the Artemis program from Space Policy Directive 1, which aims to return
humans to the Moon, marks another progressive step within the Space Age (Space Policy
Directive-1, Reinvigorating America's Human Space Exploration Program, 2017). Per this
Directive, Artemis III should deliver the next man and first woman to the Moon and will be the
first of many long-duration, deep-space, human exploration missions. These missions aim to
maintain a continuous presence on the Moon and pioneer the way to Mars(NASA’s Plan for
Sustained Lunar Exploration and Development, 2020). Such an ambitious undertaking presents
NASA and its collaborators with unique and trying technological challenges not encountered
since the Space Race. Many of these challenges stem from the harsh and dynamic environments
encountered in space, on the Moon, and on Mars. With little to no resources readily available to
support human life, planetary bases in these environments will need to be robust, independent,
and provide all the basic requirements to support life that are naturally granted on Earth.
In the case of a thirty-month mission, a single crew member (CM) will require 2250 kg of
drinking water, 6525 kg of hygiene water (oral, handwash, shower, shave, etc.), and produce
over 2000 kg of metabolic waste (Muirhead et al., 2022b; Pickett et al., 2019). With a current
payload cost of approximately $10,000/lb. (~$4,535/kg), the cost of just water for a single
astronaut exceeds $39 million (Boen, 2008). Additionally, Environmental Control and Life
Support Systems (ECLSS) technologies used in space are not optimized for Partial Gravity
Habitats and Early Planetary Base (PGH/EPB) scenarios. Urine is notoriously difficult to treat,


2
primarily due to its high nutrient and ion content, accounting for up to 80% of nitrogen found in
municipal wastewater while constituting only 1% of the total volume (Jalalieh et al.; Randall &
Naidoo, 2018; Tian et al., 2019). While the formulations and compositions of PGH/EPB waste
streams (humidity, hygiene, urine/flush, and laundry, etc.) are still under investigation, it is
estimated that urine will account for approximately 97% of the nitrogen(Muirhead & Carter,
2021; Muirhead et al., 2022b). The Water Recovery System (WRS) aboard the International
Space Station (ISS) utilizes multiple consumable inputs that are not optimized for such
applications beyond Low Earth Orbit (LEO) (Pickett et al., 2019). Consequently, the WRS is
highly dependent on a harsh chemical pretreatment method that must be constantly resupplied in
order to recover water from the urine(Pickett et al., 2019). These current challenges highlight the
need for the development of alternative treatment technologies which can operate with minimum
external inputs. For these reasons alternative treatment technologies need to be considered for
PGH/EPB scenarios. In space, treatment technologies have been strictly physical-chemical as
they provide rapid and consistent treatment (Pickett et al., 2019). However, biological treatment
systems are well established terrestrial technologies and require comparatively less input. To
date, no flight-ready biological system has been developed for space. Biological treatment is a
promising alternative for PGH/EPB as it requires less energy and inputs, leading to reduced cost
(Pickett et al., 2019).
1.2 Currently Existing Treatment Approaches for Space
The ISS is the primary testbed for current and developing ECLSS technologies. For
wastewater, the Urine Processor Assembly (UPA), Brine Processor Assembly (BPA), and Water
Processor Assembly (WPA) are the primary subsystems that make up the WRS. Prior to entering
the UPA, urine is pretreated with hexavalent chromium and phosphoric acid to prevent the


3
precipitation of uric acid and inhibit biological growth(Muirhead, 2018). Upon entering the
UPA, the pretreated urine is subjected to Vapor Compression Distillation (VCD) to separate the
water from the brine solution which recovers approximately 85% of the water found in the
urine(Carter et al., 2018; Pickett et al., 2019). The WPA achieves approximately 85% water
recovery but does not recover any nutrients (i.e., C and N) and utilizes consumables including
disposable filters and stored oxygen(Pickett et al., 2019).
A relatively recent technology, the BPA aims to treat the concentrated brine produced by
the UPA and has been on the ISS for testing since March 2021. Utilizing forced convection from
ambient air coupled with membrane distillation, water is separated from the brine as vapor. This
water vapor is recovered using the existing condensate system while the concentrated brine is
stored for disposal. Preliminary results indicate that the BPA increases the water recovered from
urine from 85% to >90%(Williamson et al., 2022). In June 2023, the WRS achieved 98% water
recovery of the currently treated waste streams (Gaskill, 2024) but still requires further
optimization. This achievement currently comes at the expense of consumable inputs (i.e.,
chemical pretreatment and filters) and loss of nutrients - .
Tied into the WRS is the Oxygen Generation System (OGS). A portion of the treated
water produced by the WPA is diverted to the Oxygen Generation Assembly (OGA). The OGA
electrolyzes the water to produce gaseous hydrogen and oxygen (Rxns. 1.1-1.3)(Takada et al.,
2021; Wang et al., 2021). The oxygen is used to revitalize the cabin air, while the hydrogen is
vented out to space or sent alongside carbon dioxide from the crew to the Carbon dioxide
Reduction System (CRS)(El Arwadi, 2023; Takada et al., 2021). Contained within the CRS, a
Sabatier reactor converts hydrogen and carbon dioxide to methane and water (Rxn. 1.4) (Yu et
al., 2022). The methane is vented overboard while the water is sent to the WPA for treatment.


