While efficient, consistent, reliable, and well documented, current space ECLSS
technologies are still being investigated for improved recovery, broader treatment capabilities,
reduced odor, and reduced consumable inputs to achieve a near closed-loop architecture(Boyce
et al., 2022; Williamson et al., 2022). The hazardous urine pretreatment chemical, hexavalent
chromium, creates safety concerns and prevents any possibility for additional resources recovery.
Recovered elements such as carbon and nitrogen will be vital for sustainable in-situ food
production. Constant use of consumable inputs (i.e., chemical pretreatment, disposable filters,
etc.) takes up valuable cargo space, requires expensive resupply missions, and creates waste that
requires disposal. Most current ISS ECLSS technologies are not suitable for EPB, as loading
requirements and waste streams are vastly different such as laundry wastewater stream.
