Chapter 2: Background
2.1 Challenges Treating Urine and Graywater
The treatment of urine presents a unique set of challenges and rewards due to its
concentration and variability. While the recovery of water and nutrients as a potential fertilizer
are considerable, treatment systems must be able to handle scaling, corrosion, fouling,
eutrophication, and inhibition caused by urine(Jurga et al., 2023; Ozair, 2012; Pourbavarsad et
al., 2022). Table 2.1 compares the characteristics of urine (fresh and hydrolyzed) and municipal
wastewater, demonstrating the extreme concentration of source separated urine compared to
what municipal technologies on Earth are designed to treat. The primary challenges stem from
the hydrolysis of urea, the main form of nitrogen in urine. Urea hydrolyses with water (Rxn. 2.1)
in the presence of the enzyme, urease, to create ammonium, bicarbonate, and hydroxide(Ray et
al., 2018). The creation of hydroxide results in a pH rise, while the ammonium and bicarbonate
increase ion concentration (electrical conductivity) and alkalinity(Ray et al., 2018).
