Complications with urine are ubiquitous in many treatment systems and environments,
making it a major subject of research (Faust et al., 2023; Larsen et al., 2021; Randall et al., 2022;
Udert et al., 2006; Udert & Wächter, 2012). Precipitation and scaling can cause blockages and
fouling in physical and chemical systems(Jurga et al., 2023; Ozair, 2012; Pourbavarsad et al.,
2022). In biological systems, the high nitrogen content often results in inhibition of
treatment(Pourbavarsad et al., 2022). The high level of nutrients (N, P, K) can cause
eutrophication in biological systems and fouling in membrane filtration systems(Akinnawo,


7
2023). The highly basic pH (>9) of hydrolyzed urine presents challenges for biological treatment
systems, which typically prefer a neutral pH (~6.5-8) (Zhou et al., 2019). To counteract the high
pH caused by hydrolyzed urine, urine hydrolysis can be prevented, or a storage system can
implement a pH control system for the hydrolyzed urine. A plethora of smaller components
including ions and nutrients found in urine contribute to its dynamic profile such as urea,
phosphate, potassium, sulfate, calcium, magnesium, as well as biological constituents, with a
comprehensive list found in Table 4.27 (Anderson et al., 2015).
Graywater includes a broad variety of wastewaters, typically shower, handwash, shave,
oral rinse, and laundry streams. Compared to blackwater, graywater generally contains less
contaminants (i.e., pathogens, organic matter, and nutrients) and, therefore, is comparatively less
hazardous, easier to treat, reuse, and recycle(Al-Jasser; Khajvand et al., 2022; Noutsopoulos et
al., 2018). However, graywater is still challenging to treat, as its characteristic profile is highly
variable. While lower in contaminants overall, graywater can contain a higher concentration of
household chemicals including surfactants which may cause foaming or inhibition of biological
treatment systems. Municipal wastewater treatment plants commonly employ biological
treatment technologies for domestic wastewater (including graywater) but often require the
addition of an external nutrient source (namely carbon/COD), as the wastewater is limited in the
nutrients required for the biological treatment consortia to achieve the desired performance(Li et
al., 2021). Generation rates of graywater are generally much higher than blackwater and can
strain the loading and treatment capability of graywater treatment systems and washout the
active organisms responsible for treatment (Filali et al., 2022).


