2.2 Treatment Approaches
2.2.1 Biological Nitrogen Removal
A considerable number of options exist to use biological technologies for waste
treatment, resource recovery, and biological nitrogen removal (BNR). Though numerous in
approaches, BNR technologies typically revolve around conversion of nitrogen within the
nitrogen cycle (Fig. 2.1). Most nitrogen in waste treatment originates from urine, accounting for
~80% found in municipal wastewater(Wuang et al., 2013). With select microorganisms and
environmental conditions, nitrogen can be converted to various forms to achieve the desired
form, typically as dinitrogen which can be off-gassed into the atmosphere. Development and use
of microorganisms for biological treatment of wastewater are typically categorized as attached or
suspended growth. Attached growth processes have the organisms growing on fixed surfaces to
accumulate a biofilm that treats surrounding wastewater and are typically used for biofilters and
biological contactors. Suspended growth cultivates organisms floating freely in solution and
treating in the mixture; they are commonly used in activated sludge systems. Some applications


9
utilize both attached and suspended growth to enhance treatment and stability but may increase
complexity and maintenance.
In Fig. 2.1, depicting the nitrogen cycle, (1) dinitrogen gas is fixed as ammonium,
commonly referred to as nitrogen fixation; (2) ammonium is oxidized by microorganisms; (3)
nitrite is oxidized to nitrate; (4) nitrate (or nitrite if not fully nitrified) is denitrified to dinitrogen
gas through a nitrous oxide intermediate; (5) anaerobic ammonium oxidation (anammox)
converts ammonia to dinitrogen; and (6) nitrate/nitrite is reduced to ammonium. (Jetten, 2008)
