One of the most common configurations of BNR for municipal use is the Modified
Ludzack Ettinger (MLE) process, which is a coupling of nitrification and denitrification
reactions to convert ammonia to nitrogen gas (Fig. 2.2a)(Metcalf & Eddy, 2003). The influent
enters an anoxic zone, where nitrogen is predominantly in the form of organic nitrogen and
ammonia. Additionally, an internal recycle introduces nitrate from downstream for nitrogen
removal via denitrification and is discussed more in the following paragraph. A relatively small
fraction of nitrogen is used in biomass uptake. Once the ammonia nitrogen enters the aerobic
stage, nitrification converts ammonia to nitrate. This is a coupled process utilizing Ammonia
Oxidizing Bacteria (AOB) that oxidizes ammonia to nitrite (Rxn. 2.2) and Nitrite Oxidizing
Bacteria (NOB) that oxidizes the nitrite into nitrate (Rxn. 2.3). The overall process is often
combined into a general nitrification process (Rxn. 2.4). These autotrophic bacteria use ammonia
and nitrite as their inorganic electron donor and oxygen as the acceptor(Baskaran et al., 2020).
Consequently, heterotrophic microorganisms oxidize organic constituents, resulting in COD
reduction and is commonly in wastewater treatment(Verma et al., 2020).
