2.2.2 Texas Tech University’s Bioreactor Systems
Although treatment technologies in space have predominantly been physical and
chemical in principle, increasing efforts have been made to develop bioregenerative technologies
as sustainable approaches. Texas Tech University (TTU) has developed a biological treatment
systems termed: Counter Diffusion Membrane Aerated Nitrifying Denitrifying Reactor or the
CoMANDR(Sevanthi et al., 2018). Based on Membrane Aerated Bioreactor (MABR)
technology, the CoMANDR has successfully treated various combinations of urine, flush,
hygiene, humidity, condensate, and laundry waste streams in pseudo ISS, transit, and EPB/PGH


13
scenarios continuously for nearly three years (Christenson et al., 2018; Sevanthi et al., 2018). As
a biological system, it can immediately treat crew generated wastes (removing the need for
intermediate storage tanks) and requires only aeration, acids, and salts as inputs (Barta et al.,
2015). While analysis and optimization of mass, volume, and energy are ongoing, challenges for
this system include biofouling mitigation, overcoming extended startup following inoculation,
improving salt rejection, mitigating water permeance, implementing automated control, and
increasing nitrogen removal as it currently achieves a maximum removal of 50%(Sevanthi et al.,
2018).
More recently, TTU and Pancopia have developed a single stage biological treatment
system (NDX) combining nitrifications, denitrification, and anammox to remove C and N
present in PGH/EPB waste streams (Fig. 2.3a) (Jalalieh et al., 2021). This system uses minimal
energy and consumable inputs to treat urine, flush, humidity condensate, hygiene, and laundry
waste streams (Jalalieh et al., 2021). The nitrogen removal ranged from 49%-66% and averaged
93% for DOC removal (Jalalieh et al., 2021). However, the system is volumetrically inefficient
and initial testing was with waste streams equivalent to less than one crew member (Jalalieh et
al., 2021).
A newer MABR based system was designed to treat crew urine and flush water
specifically. Focused on nitrification, the UMABR was designed for N conversion and C
removal (Fig. 2.3b) (Hooshyari et al., 2023; Jackson et al., 2022). Using real urine and flush, the
system was capable of achieving approximately 50% conversion of nitrogen and 90% removal of
DOC (Hooshyari et al., 2023; Jackson et al., 2022). With no immediate storage tanks needed and
efficient aeration from the membrane delivery, energy and volume requirements are reduced
(Hooshyari et al., 2023; Jackson et al., 2022). However, the system was coupled with a


14
distillation system with acid trap to prevent N carryover and HNO2 decomposition in the
distillation system (Hooshyari et al., 2023; Jackson et al., 2022). Brine solids from the distillation
process are stored/accumulated in the distillation unit (Hooshyari et al., 2023; Jackson et al.,
2022).
