2.2.3 European Space Agency’s Micro-Ecological Life Support System Alternative
One of the most prominent partnerships within the European Space Agency (ESA) led to
the development of the Micro-Ecological Life Support System Alternative (MELiSSA) (Fig.
2.4)(Walker & Granjou, 2017). Utilizing four main treatment compartments: thermophilic
anaerobic treatment, photoheterotrophic bacteria, nitrifying bacteria, and photoautotrophic


15
bacteria with higher order plants, the comprehensive system aims “to construct autonomous
habitats in deep-space, supplying astronauts with fresh air, water, and food through continuous
microbial recycling of human wastes”(Hendrickx et al., 2006; Lasseur et al., 2010; Walker &
Granjou, 2017). Waste, including urine, enters compartment one to begin treatment, but urine is
not the treatment focus until compartment three. Utilizing nitrifying bacteria in a fixed-bed
bioreactor, ammonia is nitrified into nitrate, which is the desired form to be utilized by the higher
order plants and cyanobacteria downstream in compartment four. Nitrogen conversion
efficiencies as high as 95–100% have been reported(Verbeelen et al., 2021). However, it is stated
that “[Compartment III] cannot be used to directly process urine at present”(Verbeelen et al.,
2021). Additional challenges are related to the energy efficiency primarily related to lighting
requirements for phototrophic bacteria, culturing appropriate consortia tolerant of a dynamic
ammonia influent, and lack of architecture integration(Hendrickx et al., 2006).
