2.2.4 CO2 Carbonation
A primary problem in urine treatment, aside from inhibition caused by high nitrogen
content, is the increase in pH when it hydrolyzes. Since the desired pH range for AOB and NOB
is 6–7.5, inhibition from pH is also likely(Sharif Shourjeh et al., 2021). Traditionally acid
addition is used when pH reduction is necessary; however, if a solution has a high alkalinity,
(like hydrolyzed urine) it can require substantial amounts of acid. In the case of space treatment,
the need for large volumes of acid would reduce the sustainability of a bioregenerative treatment
technology(Udert & Wächter, 2012). The addition of dissolved CO2 is a novel method that has
been demonstrated to reduce pH in urine(Courtney et al., 2021; Randall et al., 2022). As shown
in Rxn. 2.10, gaseous CO2 dissolves and becomes aqueous, then reacts with water to form
carbonic acid (Rxn. 2.11). The carbonic acid then dissociates to form bicarbonate and releases a
hydrogen ion (Rxn. 2.12. The bicarbonate also dissociates to produce carbonate and release
another hydrogen ion (Rxn. 2.13). The release of these hydrogen ions contributes to the
reduction of pH. The production of these carbonate species, especially calcium carbonate (Rxn.
2.14), are some of the primary constituents that contribute to the alkalinity of a solution
