MBRs are ideal for various applications including remote and space restricted
environments due to their retention of biomass and can provide high quality treatment with an
absolute barrier provided by the membrane. This has been demonstrated in multiple cases, such
as the development of a Non-Sewered Sanitation (NSS) system termed the Nutrients, Energy,
and Water generator (NEWgenerator or NEWgen). Centered around an AnMBR, the NEWgen
couples it with ion exchange and electro-chlorination to form a decentralized treatment system
capable of meeting ISO 30500 requirements and is summarized by Fig. 2.6(Bair et al., 2015;
Shyu et al., 2021; Watabe et al., 2023). Long-term field tests in India and South Africa
demonstrated NEWgen’s capability to treat a wide variety of wastes, achieve high resource
recovery, consistently produce a high-quality product water, all in a self-sustaining manner in
remote environments(Bair et al., 2015; Shyu et al., 2021; Watabe et al., 2023).
NEWgen’s success as a NSS system in remote applications influenced the development
of a MBR system as a bioregenerative life support technology for space applications. Also
centered around an AnMBR, the Organic Processor Assembly (OPA), was designed to
sustainably treat the fecal waste generated by a crew of four during long-duration, deep-space


