2.3 Statement of Research
To advance the limited field of sustainable ECLSS technologies for future exploration, an
MBR-based system is proposed as a bioregenerative alternative for the treatment and recovery of
key resources in urine and graywater. Fig. 2.8 depicts how SAMBR is envisioned to integrate
into future ECLSS architecture. Using conditioning, BNR, and membrane filtration technologies,
this hybrid system is termed the SAMBR. As mass and volume constraints are paramount for
space exploration, a small footprint will be required for this technology. Reduced consumable
inputs will be needed to make this system competitive against similar technologies. As MLE and
4-stage Bardenpho configurations are typically industrial scale biological treatment processes,
scaling down will be necessary and is not known to have been done for space applications. The
process of developing and converting a series of industrial scale terrestrial technologies to small,
intensive, and concentrated treatment in space will expand the known boundary of space waste
treatment. The research presented here aims to investigate SAMBR’s capability to address many
of these challenges and to be a progressive step towards a sustainable ECLSS architecture.
To assess the feasibility of developing SAMBR for space applications, the following
questions were investigated during the course of this research:
1. What will be required to achieve optimal influent conditioning and what will the
effects be throughout the system?
2. Are there additional inhibiting factors that the influent conditioning subsystem is
unable to address and how might these be addressed in the future?
3. What design parameters for the system process and architecture are needed to meet
the restraints for space while maintaining a sufficient hydraulic retention time (HRT)
to adequately treat the urine and hygiene waste streams generated by a crew of four?


22
4. How will the biological consortia respond to increasing concentrations of crew waste,
namely nitrogen (as ammonia)?
5. What will be the maximum amount of nutrient conversion and removal the system
can achieve? And how will this compare against similar technologies?
6. What will be fates of influent COD and nitrogen throughout the system?
The associated research tasks are as follows:
1. Investigate the development of subsystems to condition the urine and graywater
influent to create ideal conditions for the biological consortia.
2. Using NASA designated design constraints, design the system process and
architecture for a maximum HRT while maintaining a footprint within said
constraints.
3. Fabricate a model housing and bioreactor, then integrate the bioreactor and associated
hardware into the housing.
4. Hydraulicly test the system to verify design, assembly, and viability of the biological
consortia.
5. Operate the system with increasing strengths of urine for consortia activity until the
full-strength of urine and flush is achieved.
6. Utilizing a modeling approach, assess the fate of COD and nitrogen throughout the
system.
The overall objective is to design, develop, and evaluate the SAMBR concept for its
potential to provide a bioregenerative alternative for treatment of urine and graywater in space.
